+# Request-Derived Values Reach Template Links as Link-Expression Parameters
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+Every paging link in the application is built the same way: a literal URL with the page number spliced in by expression preprocessing, written `__${currentPage + 1}__`. Preprocessing substitutes the evaluated value into the link expression's text, and the engine then parses the result as an expression.
+
+Page numbers are the application's own values, so what gets spliced is always a number the controller produced.
+
+`REQ-VET-003` requires the paging links of the veterinarian directory to carry the specialty filter, which is text an arbitrary caller supplies. Copying the surrounding idiom would splice caller text into an expression before parsing — the injection is into the template engine, not into the page, so output escaping does not reach it.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Copy the preprocessing idiom for the filter.** Rejected: it turns a request parameter into template-expression source. This is a demonstration application, and the idiom would be read and copied.
+2. **Escape or validate the filter before splicing.** Rejected: it keeps a dangerous construction and defends it with a filter, against a syntax with no simple safe subset.
+3. **Restrict the filter to the known specialty names.** Rejected: it protects this one link by adding a matching rule ahead of the query, and leaves the next link unprotected.
+4. **Build the link with link-expression parameters** (chosen), written `@{/vets.html(page=${i},specialty=${specialty})}`. The engine appends each value as a URL-encoded query parameter and never parses it as an expression.
+
+## Decision
+
+A request-derived value reaches a link only as a link-expression parameter. Preprocessing stays available for values the application computes, which is what the existing paging links use it for.
+
+The existing links are not rewritten by this decision. They splice only page numbers, and no caller value reaches them.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The filter survives paging without the caller's text ever entering an expression.
+- The engine handles encoding, so a specialty name containing a space or an ampersand round-trips correctly.
+- The directory's paging links mix two idioms — parameters on the filtered links, preprocessing elsewhere in the file. The rule above says which applies where.
+- Every later link carrying a request value has a pattern to follow, and a reviewer has a bright line to judge against.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-VET-003
+
+## References
+
+- [system-design.md § View Templates](../system-design.md#view-templates) — the current-state rule.
+- [system-design.md § Threat Model](../system-design.md#threat-model) — the expression-injection row.
+- [security-principles.md § Realization](../security-principles.md#realization) — the cross-site-scripting row this sits beside; that row governs rendered output, this decision governs expression construction.
